at its early access baldur's gate 3 has  six starter classes available  and classes matter a lot more than they  did in larian's previous rpg divinity  original sin 2. but it's also not as  simple as choosing your virtual job  either  as the baldur's gate 3 character creator  follows a lot of the same rules as  classic dungeons and dragons  your character's race and the abilities  that go alongside your bloodline  also play a huge part in your build so i  thought i'd go through some of the most  effective combinations for each class  and race combo  from the start of the game up until your  third level for all those out there who  want to min max their experience  for fighters strength and constitution  are the stats to pay most attention to  starting as a gith yankee is an okay  choice as they have that plus two  strength  but i think the best choice is to go for  the shield wharf as they also have a  plus two constitution modifier on top of  their strength constitution being the  stat that affects health  so by combining both of those you have  an effective frontline melee character  who can also soak up a bit of damage but  also think about what weapons you want  to start with  dwarves obviously favor axes and hammers  while gith  are more effective with swords the  strength modifier is also going to get  you into melee combat more effectively  as it's the strength stat that affects  how far you can jump  which is a great tactic to get you in  and out of combat  the first level up is when you can start  to really build your fighter around a  roll  as this is when you can start taking  your traits do you want to focus on  protection  great weapons dual wielding etc  personally if i want to deal damage i  will go for the dual wielding option as  it will allow you to diversify the  damage output between different weapon  types and status effects  if you want a tank to take the damage  away from your squishy allies then i  recommend going down the protection  route and start planning ahead  to add some shield proficiencies this  level up will also get you an action  surge giving you an extra action with  every use  so be ready for those double hits and on  your next level up when you hit level  three  you get to pick between battlemaster or  eldritch knight going the eldritch  knight route basically makes you  a battle mage but with the current  roster you're already likely to have  some spellcasters in party  so i recommend keeping the focus on  melee that's where the battlemaster  option excels with some really good  passives  my advice is to swap out pushing attacks  and take riposte instead  repost grants you the ability to counter  attack which can be super effective with  two weapon fighting styles and the  downside to pushing attack is that it  has a chance to push  enemies away firstly if you're the tank  you want the enemies near you  and secondly it means you lose out on  the possibility of a reactionary attack  if the enemy chooses to flee away from  you  rangers have the option to choose their  favored enemy and an option for their  natural explorer perk a lot of this  feels like it will be more useful beyond  the act one which is what's included  in early access personally i think  players should either go for mage  breaker  which gives access to true strike  kantrip and some experience in arcana  for the second option i chose beast  tamer because it allows you to take on a  familiar these are pretty small  and squishy but it can be a good  distraction for enemies  as well as having some fairly decent  status effects like poison  or blindness be aware though at level 3  this is going to be pretty much  completely replaced  by something far better one strong  choice for race is wood elf for their  high dexterity and fleet of foot  abilities  but i think the better option if you  want to play that kind of stealth sniper  role is the lightfoot halfling  as they have high dexterity are stealthy  and have the race ability of lucky which  allows them to re-roll all ones  which means critical fails will be an  extremely rare sight for you  your first level up should be focused on  archery as the rest of your options here  are going to be done  much better by pretty much any other  party member you're taking with you  as for spells the most useful is going  to be ensnaring strike  keeping enemies a distance while you do  some damage and my other option of  choice is speak with animals because  honestly with all the story potential  you get from that ability it should be a  no-brainer to pick  whenever you have the option and for the  second level up the best ability to pick  is beastmaster now this is going to  allow you to summon larger animal  companions like bears and wolves into  combat  the options this gives you are great as  the summons are another damage dealer  with its own unique ability  and it's another massive entity to soak  up damage for essentially free  helping ensure the main party members  stay healthy  warlock is my favorite class it has such  a variety of options and weird spells  i think everyone should build one at  least once now for warlocks the primary  stat to pay attention to is charisma  as this is what they're going to use to  cast spells but high charisma is also  going to be super helpful when  interacting with npcs  outside of combat t flings and half  elves both have that plus two charisma  stat but as much as i love tieflings i  think half elf is a stronger option  as they also get an extra point to put  into two other stats of their choice  further helping diversify your warlock  you're going to have fewer spell slots  as a warlock so it's worth thinking  about how you're going to rely on your  cantrips and other basic weaponry  when it comes to your packs you have two  options you could opt into the great old  one  if you want battlefield control and  disables but if you want more melee  utility the fiend  will be a better choice personally i  went the strange cthulhu style route  as for cantrips i think the only really  essential one is eldritch blast  it's basically going to be your bread  and butter for ranged damage  and the more you level up the more  options you have to upgrade it dissonant  whispers and hideous laughter are both  great options for burst damage and  disabling your enemies for a few turns  and at your first level up you'll notice  there are a lot of options that relate  to things you already have i recommend  at least taking agonizing blasts to  upgrade the damage of eldritch blast  as like i said you're going to be using  that ability a lot  and beast speech again because it lets  you talk to animals and that's  never not been great spell choice will  depend on how you've built so far  whether you're going to focus on melee  range damage or control  charm person will help out here and  there and battle against humanoids but  for an option that's pretty much  always usable taking the hex enchantment  as a bonus  will make the rest of your attack sting  that little bit more level 3 grants  access to pact of the chain another  summoning ability  although rather than the normal  familiars you get to pick from imps or  quasits  much more powerful with much more potent  poisons than your average familiars  and for my final spell choice i'd say  either misty step for keeping you on the  high ground or if you feel like you need  a little aoe  shatter is a great option but again when  it comes to spells is really going to be  down to how you've played that character  so far  rogues feel like one of the classes that  aren't quite there yet when it comes to  what abilities and options are available  to you  but if you're playing one then i think  taking drow as your race is going to  give you the most benefits  as they both have plus two in dexterity  decent weapon proficiencies  and are the absolute best at seeing in  the dark which is going to be great for  all the  skulking around and backstabbing you're  likely to do one thing you should bear  in mind is that you need to be getting  behind enemies as much as possible  to take advantage of sneak attacks your  first level up is going to help you  there  as it's going to allow you to take dash  as a bonus action meaning you can rush  behind an enemy and still have the  ability to plunge your daggers in if you  want to mix things up come level 3 you  can add some spell casting by taking  arcane trickster  but again as you're likely to have a  spellcaster or two already at this point  i say keep to what you know and go for  the thief option  and continue to focus on stabbing spines  the thief choice will also mean you take  less fall damage if you're knocked from  your high ground hiding spots but fast  hands is the biggest bonus as it gives  you an  extra bonus action so that's either an  extra extra dash or more opportunities  to attack with your offhand weapon  just remember to stay mobile and keep  some melee backup or take buffs from  your mages as you're going to be a great  damage dealer  but awfully squishy with all the spells  wizards have as  options an exact build won't be that  helpful but i can tell you  what's going to give you the best head  start unlike every other class and race  combo there's nothing that will give you  a plus two intelligence trait which is  the wizard spell casting stat of choice  going human is fine as you get a few  other stat boosts but personally i think  the extra can trip you get from going  high elf  is much more appealing also elves are  immune from being charmed or put to  sleep  so this will ensure you can keep your  spell casters slinging lightning and  also a bit of elven dexterity for  dodging attacks  never hurt anyone i opted to swap out  grease and fog for thunder wave to push  away any bad guys melee rushing me  and magic missile as it's an extremely  reliable and consistent damage dealer  for the early game  first level up is basically a choice  between if you're looking to protect  yourself or deal some damage  evocation spells focus elemental energy  into powerful attacks and enchantments  and ab duration spells summon wards  banish enemies and nullify magic  evocation means you'll lose out on some  of the arcade ward abilities  and have to be a bit more precious with  your spell slots but you do get the  sculpt spells passive meaning that your  allies won't take damage from all your  spell flinging  so you can fire away with burning hands  with a bit more reckless abandon like i  said a full build guide isn't going to  help much for wizards especially when  they have  25 spells to choose from a level 3 so  what you pick here is likely just going  to be based on what you've enjoyed so  far  it is worth noting though that even if a  spell isn't in this list you can always  pay to learn it later  if you find a spell scroll and a handful  of gold although i must say again  for misty step having that is always a  bonus keeps you on the high ground and  away from  most of the danger i think the best bet  if you're going to play cleric is to  focus on life domain this is going to  make you an extremely powerful healer  and dealer of buffs also if you take  shadow heart along with you she's a  cleric of the opposite school  so you'd already be covered anyway i  opted to play as wood elf as it gave me  the biggest boost to wisdom  one of the most important stats for  clerics which is going to be very  helpful in the early game as you start  out with a very powerful spell and can  trip combo  guiding bolt and sacred flame the extra  stats are best served in constitution  and dexterity  especially if you're going with that  melee shield build that is so common  with clerics  these stats are just going to serve you  that much better if you're a frontline  battle party member the progression over  both levels is pretty linear  your focus should just be on buffs  protection spells and healing  as the other companions are going to be  pretty lacking in that department  by level 3 you should be able to keep  everyone topped up with hp via cure  wounds aid preserve life and the prayer  of healing and with the slots you have  spare taking spells like halt and hold  person will keep foes at bay  giving you a turn to dish out some heels  those are my choices for the most meta  or  min maxed builds to start you off in  baldur's gate 3. but honestly though the  character you should build  is the one you most want to play there  is absolutely nothing wrong with playing  a githyanki cleric a dwarven mage  or a human fighter it's a role-playing  game with such depth and creativity that  despite this build guide the best piece  of advice i can give you  is to experiment and be creative but  regardless i hope you enjoyed this video  if you did hit the like button and let  me know your favorite class builds in  baldur's gate 3  and what classes and races do you hope  larian adds next  until the next video you can find me on  twitter at irregular dave  and i'll catch you in the next one  [Music]  [Music]

